meeting in a 9-3 vote, with three members favoring a 25-basis-point hike amid core PCE inflation at 3.3% for June and a still-resilient labor market. Headline CPI eased to 3.5% year-over-year in June from 4.2% in May on softer energy prices, while core CPI fell to 2.6%, yet both remain well above the 2% target. Geopolitical tensions tied to energy supply have added upside risks, shifting market-implied odds toward potential tightening at the September 15-16 FOMC meeting. Traders are closely watching the July CPI release on August 12 and the Jackson Hole symposium later in the month for signals on whether incoming data will sustain or ease hawkish pressure.","context_requires_regen":false,"context_updated_at":"2026-08-11T13:46:37.058Z"},"version":"v1"}]
